{
  "artifact_type": "development_exposed_repo_scale_feature_ceiling",
  "date": "2026-07-29",
  "claim_bearing": false,
  "supersedes": "The first version of this file, committed in 4ca04bb, was WRONG. Its causal arms omitted the task settlement barrier, so a clause could see earlier clauses of its own task. Agents re-run the same command within a task, so the leak was concentrated exactly in the repository-by-binary arm this file was assessing. All causal arms are restated below; the ORACLE arms are hindsight over all rows and are barrier-independent, so they are unchanged.",
  "correction_magnitude": {
    "SWE277_CPU_repo_bin_utility": {
      "as_committed": 1790,
      "corrected": 100
    },
    "SWE277_CPU_bin_utility": {
      "as_committed": 629,
      "corrected": 333
    },
    "SWE100_CPU_repo_bin_utility": {
      "as_committed": 564,
      "corrected": 24
    },
    "SWE277_RSS_repo_bin_utility": {
      "as_committed": 241,
      "corrected": -2
    }
  },
  "question": "Is a repository-scale feature -- repository size, file count, test count, anything needing a checkout -- worth collecting?",
  "method": "Any repo-scale feature is a function of the repository, so a hindsight per-repository Heavy-rate oracle upper-bounds every such feature at once. Causal arms enforce the task settlement barrier.",
  "scoring": {
    "theta": 0.16666666666666666,
    "utility": "5*TP - 1*FP",
    "cost_ratio_B_to_C": "5:1 declared by the human"
  },
  "answer": {
    "verdict": "still do not collect, but for a WEAKER reason than first stated",
    "corrected_reasoning": "The original claim -- that the repository oracle loses to command-only everywhere -- does not survive correction. With the barrier enforced the repository oracle BEATS command-only for CPU on SWE277 (588 vs 333) and for RSS on SWE277 (112 vs -10), because correcting the leak deflated the command-only arm too. It still loses for CPU on SWE100 (115 vs 135). So a repository-level feature has a real but COHORT-INCONSISTENT ceiling, and would fail the two-orientation consistency any gate here requires.",
    "unchanged_and_structural": "The repository oracle is exactly zero for Disk in BOTH cohorts and for RSS on SWE100, because no repository's Heavy rate reaches theta at all: Disk per-repository Heavy rate peaks at 12.9% and 12.8% against a 1/6 cut. This is hindsight and barrier-independent, so it stands as originally reported."
  },
